def two_sum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:
    num_dict = {}
    for i, num in enumerate(nums):
        complement = target - num
        if complement in num_dict:
            return [num_dict[complement], i]
        num_dict[num] = i
    # The problem states there's exactly one solution, so no need for error handling
```

This function uses a hash map (dictionary) to store each number's index as it iterates through the list. For each number, it calculates the complement (target - current number) and checks if this complement exists in the dictionary. If found, it returns the indices of the complement and the current number. This ensures an O(n) time complexity with O(n) space complexity, efficiently finding the solution as required.
